# DESCRIPTION

A pager VMO is a VMO whose pages come from a process, the pager: a file service, say, whose VMOs are files.
When a page the VMO lacks is needed, because a thread faulted on it, or a commit, a pin or a read through a child reaches it, the kernel queues a request on the VMO's port and the needing thread waits until the pager supplies the page.
The request is a `CROI_PKT_TYPE_PAGE_REQUEST` packet (port(2)) with the key given at creation, and in its payload the command `CROI_PAGER_VMO_READ`, `CROI_PAGER_VMO_DIRTY` or `CROI_PAGER_VMO_COMPLETE` in the low 16 bits of payload[0] (flags above), the offset in payload[1] and the length in payload[2].
Requests that overlap are joined, so the pager gets one packet for a range.
Pages supplied are clean; a clean page is mapped read only, so the first write faults.

With `CROI_VMO_TRAP_DIRTY` the first write to a clean page also asks the pager, with a `CROI_PAGER_VMO_DIRTY` request, and waits for the pager's `CROI_PAGER_OP_DIRTY`.
Without it the page is simply marked dirty.
A pager writes dirty pages back with `CROI_PAGER_OP_WRITEBACK_BEGIN` and `_END`.
A page of a pager VMO that is clean may be evicted by the kernel under memory pressure; the pager is asked again when it is needed.

The calls that take a VMO need it to be backed by the pager given (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS` otherwise).

## pager_create

`pager_create` writes a handle to a new pager to <out>.
<options> must be 0.
The handle has INSPECT, TRANSFER, ATTACH_VMO and MANAGE_VMO.
The job's policy for new pagers applies.
When the last handle to a pager closes, every VMO it backs is detached.

## pager_create_vmo

`pager_create_vmo` writes to <out> a handle to a VMO of <size> bytes backed by <pager>, which needs ATTACH_VMO.
Requests are queued on <port>, which needs WRITE, with key <key>.
<options> is any of `CROI_VMO_RESIZABLE`, `CROI_VMO_UNBOUNDED` (as for vmo(2)) and `CROI_VMO_TRAP_DIRTY`; `CROI_VMO_DISCARDABLE` and anything else is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The handle has the rights of a vmo_create handle, with RESIZE if the VMO is resizable.
The job's policy for new VMOs applies.

## pager_detach_vmo

`pager_detach_vmo` ends the pager's service of <vmo>.
The pager handle needs ATTACH_VMO and MANAGE_VMO, the VMO's WRITE.
Every thread waiting for a page is woken with `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`, clean pages are dropped, and a `CROI_PAGER_VMO_COMPLETE` request is queued once.
The same happens when the VMO's last reference goes.
Dirty pages stay, for the pager to write back.

## pager_supply_pages

`pager_supply_pages` moves the pages of [<aux_offset>, <aux_offset> + <length>) of <aux_vmo> into [<offset>, <offset> + <length>) of <vmo>, where the VMO has none.
The pager handle needs MANAGE_VMO, <vmo> WRITE and <aux_vmo> READ and WRITE.
<offset>, <length> and <aux_offset> are page aligned.
<aux_vmo> must be an anonymous VMO that is no one's child and no pager's (`CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`) with the range inside it (`CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`) and no page pinned (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`).
The pages are moved, not copied: <aux_vmo> loses them; where it had none, zeros are supplied.
Threads waiting for them go on.
A detached VMO is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`.

## pager_op_range

`pager_op_range` does <op> on [<offset>, <offset> + <length>) of <vmo>, both page aligned and inside the VMO (`CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`).
The pager handle needs MANAGE_VMO and <vmo> WRITE.

: `CROI_PAGER_OP_FAIL`
  Fails the requests in the range: <data> is the status the waiting threads get, one of `CROI_ERR_IO`, `CROI_ERR_IO_DATA_INTEGRITY`, `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`, `CROI_ERR_NO_SPACE` or `CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL` (else `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`).
: `CROI_PAGER_OP_DIRTY`
  Answers a dirty request: marks the pages dirty, and releases the waiting threads.
  <data> is 0.
  Every page must be present (`CROI_ERR_NOT_FOUND`).
  The VMO must have been made with TRAP_DIRTY (`CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`).
: `CROI_PAGER_OP_WRITEBACK_BEGIN`
  The dirty pages in the range await clean, and are unmapped so that a write meanwhile dirties them again.
  <data> is 0, or `CROI_VMO_DIRTY_RANGE_IS_ZERO` when the pager writes the range as zeros.
: `CROI_PAGER_OP_WRITEBACK_END`
  The pages still awaiting clean become clean.
  <data> is 0.

Any other <op> is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

## pager_query_dirty_ranges

`pager_query_dirty_ranges` writes to <buffer> the runs of pages in [<offset>, <offset> + <length>) that are not clean, in order, each a `croi_vmo_dirty_range_t` of 24 bytes (offset, length and options, `CROI_VMO_DIRTY_RANGE_IS_ZERO` for grown space never written).
The range is rounded out to pages.
The first argument packs the pager handle in its low 32 bits, which needs MANAGE_VMO, and the VMO's in its high 32, which needs READ.
As many runs are written as <buffer_size> holds.
<out>, if not null, receives two `uint64_t`: how many were written and how many there are.

## pager_query_vmo_stats

`pager_query_vmo_stats` writes a `croi_pager_vmo_stats_t` to <buffer>, whose `modified` field is `CROI_PAGER_VMO_STATS_MODIFIED` if the VMO has been written since the last reset.
<buffer_size> must hold it (`CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`).
<options> is 0 or `CROI_PAGER_RESET_VMO_STATS`, which clears the flag.
The pager handle needs MANAGE_VMO and the VMO READ.

